    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "42ba36fe_ee9b36e6",
        "filename": "chip/g/rdd.c",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 188,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000039
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-08-23T02:37:45Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I am very confused now: function rdd_is_detected() returning true does not actually mean that rdd is detected?\n\nCould this function be unreliable when it is used in rdd ISR?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 188,
        "startChar": 5,
        "endLine": 188,
        "endChar": 20
      },
      "revId": "cbbf323aa16cbba810de1a29d0cd4405e6da9e51",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2b5a785a_1abf025f",
        "filename": "chip/g/rdd.c",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 188,
      "author": {
        "id": 1136059
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-08-23T17:19:38Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Kind of. rdd_is_detected returns true if the cc lines are in the correct voltage range. Not if it is a valid connected state.\n\nWhen the ISR is triggered, the CC lines have been stable for some time. I think that means they\u0027d probably remain stable for a little bit longer.\n\nIf it is unreliable and there is an incorrect read on rdd_is_detected, the map would be set to detect the opposite state, and I think that means the interrupt would trigger again and the second read would correct it.",
      "parentUuid": "42ba36fe_ee9b36e6",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 188,
        "startChar": 5,
        "endLine": 188,
        "endChar": 20
      },
      "revId": "cbbf323aa16cbba810de1a29d0cd4405e6da9e51",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d8842021_dd9f324f",
        "filename": "chip/g/rdd.c",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 188,
      "author": {
        "id": 1136059
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-08-23T17:29:02Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I did an experiment reading cc1, cc2, RDD_CUR_STABLE_STATE_DEBUG, and RDD_CUR_STABLE_STATE_INVALID in rdd_is_detected. \n\nIn the interrupt rdd_is_detected read STATE_DEBUG would be 1 and STATE_INVALID would be 0. cc1 and cc2 were 3 and 3 for connected. 0 and 0 or 7 and 7 for disconnected.\n\nI disconnected all adapters and suzyq from the DUT.\n\nCr50 goes through the rdd_detect hook once a second. Normally the cc lines would be 0 or 7 the INVALID bit would be 1 and the DEBUG bit would be 0. Occasionally I would see that the cc lines were 3, but the DEBUG bit was 0 and the STABLE bit was 1. This change just ignores that state in rdd_detect, so we can wait for it to become stable or return to the disconnected state.",
      "parentUuid": "2b5a785a_1abf025f",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 188,
        "startChar": 5,
        "endLine": 188,
        "endChar": 20
      },
